YAAAP: Yet another atheist ascension post.

Nabiki the Chaotic Human Wizard, as her first communication with Anhur,
sacrificed the Amulet of Yendor after 76,767 turns.  With her
original pet in tow.

There are certain combinations of classes and challenges that just
seem right: vegetarian, celibate monks, genocideless healers,
illiterate cavemen, wishless samurai, and, to me, at least, atheist 
wizards.  Nabiki was my third try at this, and first success.

This game had one of the best starts I've ever seen, statwise:
Int 18, Con 15 (albeit, this implied STR 7, DEX 8, but that's not all
that bad, imho).  Plus, in her starting inventory was a blessed potion 
of gain ability *gulp* Con 16.  Oh, and a +3 ring of gain constitution,
make that Con 19 after 2 moves.  Pretty spiffy for a wizard.  Hit 
points were never much of a concern for Nabiki.

She let her pet feline Warren get most of the kills for the first
section of the game; he quickly grew up to be a large cat.  On
Dlev 5, she found a long wand.  Hmmm, wonder what this does...
engrave, "For what do you wish?".  Okay then.  Blessed scrolls
of charging, SDSM, ring of slow digestion (atheism), cornuthaum,
spellbook of identify, and speed boots.  

On to the mines.  I had to restrain myself from #chatting with 
the priest, with 1,000 zorkmids in my pocket at level 2.  The
shops had not much of interest, although the guards did offer up
their normal booty of gloves.  

Two levels below minetown, Warren resolutely failed to answer to
my (tin) whistle on the downstairs.  I put on my blindfold, no white
"f" to be seen.  Poor Warren. 

The rest of the mines were as usual.  Luckstone, keys, lamps, gems, the
normal stuff.  On my way back up, I move to fight a dingo..."Are
you sure you want to attack Warren?"  Yay!  Warren lives!  And
there's probably a polytrap around here, too!  Why, looky here!
Warren the baby white dragon was tempting, but one more displacement
yielded Warren the master mind flayer.  Perhaps a bit scary, but 
worth it.

The mines had yielded a nice set of 36 daggers*, now properly 
rustproofed.  Still no holy water, so I held off on the enchantment.
Even so, they did nice work, even against dragons and Croesus.  I used 
them regularly until I found the VS (they got enchanted post-castle).  
After that, I had to conserve inventory weight in the endgame.

*In my opinion, this is the main reason, apart from protection, that the
Mines are worth it.

Back up to the main dungeon, letting Warren do most of the work,
picking up various armor and weapons along the way.  I decided
to skip the quest, since, as an atheist, I would have a lot of 
difficulty obtaining any artifacts except by wishing, so I'd use
the castle wand to get Magicbane first.  Ludios was as per usual,
except that I killed many fewer soldiers than normal, as Warren
was feasting on their brains the whole time.  

It turned out that I was just a bit underprepared for the castle (AC 
-4), but a bit of caution, a fortuitous rubber chicken, and Warren
got me to the wand.

I did have a very close call coming out the the castle; a ki-rin
ambushed me, summoned a bunch of nasties, which then proceeded to
thrash me within an inch of my life.  I quaffed my entire collection
of healing potions, and managed to make it to the upstairs.  Whereupon,
my first move was to walk directly onto a hole.  Sigh.  Fortunately,
the nasties surrounding me weren't all that scary, and Warren and I
were up and away.  Buried in the booty was a blessed scroll of remove
curse, so now I had holy water.  Sweet.  AC -20.  Castle wishes (0:1)
went for Magicbane, 2x blessed magic marker, 1x cursed magic marker,
and blessed scrolls of genocide.  Polished off Sokoban (reflection,
natch), then did the nurse dance therein.  Genocided the insta-Warren
killers (w,t), the annoying critters (X,P,R,U), the summoner 
critters (L), and the Orcus-shop stealers (m).  I don't usually
genocide, but, since I was playing an atheist, I decided to indulge.
Liberally.  Decided against genociding 'h', though, as I had grown
rather attached to Warren.

Naturally, a level 16 wizard with an AC of -20, 290 hp, the magic 
missile spell, and a pet level 19 MMF waltzed through the quest.
Got a genocide from the throne, winged gargoyles.

Gehennom, Gehennom, Gehennom.  Let Warren eat the priest in the
VotD.  Nothing out of the ordinary, collect stuff, kill the lords,
kill Vlad (this guy really needs to be tougher), find the VS (took a 
while, though, it was well hidden).  And my original pet was still with 
me.  Good old Warren.  I fixed him up with some nicely enchanted 
armor*, and tried to get him to wield a +7 mattock, but he seemed to
prefer his spear.  Okay, Warren, whatever.  He did, however, accept
the Sokoban amulet of reflection.  Neat, now I don't have to worry
about errant magic missiles.  At one point, Warren was Level 19,
HP 152(152), AC -26, tame, fast, invisible.  (Also flying and reflecting,
of course).  That's a nice pet.

* j - a blessed +5 dwarvish mitril coat named Flayerwear, and so on.

Buff up for Rodney.  Realize I didn't have enough candles (there's 
another post about that), fix it via collection and polypiling.  My 
stats by now were pretty much what you see below.  Zip, zap, ding dong, 
Rodney's dead.  

Left Warren outside the Sanctum, so he wouldn't get hurt when I used 
conflict.  Cast magic missile 'til my ears bleed, zap the preist with 
a wand of death (no FoD spell this game), snatch the amulet, collect 
Warren, and  start the ascent.  Boring, but fast.  Got thrown back a 
few times, but  not enough to be more than a slight annoyance.  Rodney 
showed up once, and double troubled.  Magic missles took care of him. 

The earth plane was easy, the air plane was easy once I remembered
about hanging out inside of steam vortices, fire was easy, water
took a long time to find the portal, and drained lots of power from
magic missiling the krakens.  Warren wasn't much good in the planes,
but I'd brought him this far, and I wasn't going to abandon him in the
Plane of Fire, with all those nasty elementals.

Made it to the Astral, decided to try Pestilence, wand of death'ed
him, fought through the insects, priests, and adventurers, it was the 
right altar, collected Warren, end of game.


An invisible choir sings, and you are bathed in radiance...--More--


